i. Information Maintenance Commands
wc: Word Count. Shows lines, words, and bytes in a file or input. Example: wc filename

clear: Clears the terminal screen.

cal: Displays a calendar for the current month or specified year/month. Example: cal 2025

who: Shows who is currently logged in.

date: Displays or sets the system date and time. Example: date

pwd: Prints the current working directory.

ii. File Management Commands
cat: Concatenates and displays file contents. Example: cat file.txt

cp: Copies files or directories. Example: cp source.txt dest.txt

rm: Removes files or directories. Example: rm file.txt

mv: Moves or renames files or directories. Example: mv oldname.txt newname.txt

cmp: Compares two files byte by byte. Example: cmp file1 file2

comm: Compares two sorted files line by line. Example: comm file1 file2

diff: Shows differences between files. Example: diff file1 file2

find: Searches for files in a directory hierarchy. Example: find /path -name filename

grep: Searches for patterns in files. Example: grep 'pattern' file

awk: Pattern scanning and processing language used for text filtering and data extraction. Example: awk '{print $1}' file

iii. Directory Management Commands
cd: Changes the directory. Example: cd /home/user

mkdir: Creates directories. Example: mkdir newdir

rmdir: Removes empty directories. Example: rmdir olddir

ls: Lists directory contents. Example: ls -l
